WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.223
Cloud PKI gives you two ways to anchor
trust, and both can live in one tenant.

2
00:00:05.223 --> 00:00:09.477
With a Cloud PKI root, you create
the whole hierarchy in the cloud,

3
00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:12.843
but every relying party has
to be given the new root.

4
00:00:12.843 --> 00:00:17.834
With bring your own CA, you create an
issuing CA in the cloud and your existing

5
00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:22.825
private CA signs it, so the chain relying
parties already trust is preserved.

6
00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:26.390
A few details matter, like
making sure the signing

7
00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:29.784
CA publishes its chain
over HTTP, not just LDAP.

8
00:00:29.784 --> 00:00:32.329
But one consequence outweighs the rest.

9
00:00:32.329 --> 00:00:36.729
Under bring your own CA, the private
root's CRL stays where it is,

10
00:00:36.729 --> 00:00:41.218
and every relying party, Entra ID
included, still has to fetch it.

11
00:00:41.218 --> 00:00:44.590
A root that publishes late
breaks the cloud issuer too.
